# =============================================================================
#  .dockerignore -- BUILD-CONTEXT CONTAINMENT FOR THE COMPILED-COBOL ORACLE
# =============================================================================
#
#  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
#  --------------------
#  Both harness images declare `context: ..`, i.e. the REPOSITORY ROOT
#  [harness/docker-compose.yml] -- which is mandatory, because
#  [harness/Dockerfile.mariadb:L301] copies `mysql/ACASDB.sql` and
#  [harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol] copies `requirements*.txt`,
#  `presql2-latest.zip` and `mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip`. Every one of
#  those paths resolves only relative to the repository root.
#
#  A repository-root context, with no exclusions, hands the ENTIRE checkout to
#  the daemon -- including the git history, any local virtual environment and
#  any credential file an operator happens to have left in the tree. Measured
#  on this checkout, that is roughly 284 MB of material no image needs:
#  `.git` (~158 MB), `.venv` (~122 MB) and `.blitzy_tmp` (~3.8 MB).
#
#  Two distinct harms follow, and this file addresses both:
#
#    1. DISCLOSURE. Anything in the context can be read by the build, can be
#       captured in an image layer by an unintended COPY, and is transmitted to
#       whatever daemon performs the build -- which is not necessarily local.
#       A local `.env`, a private key or a shell history in the tree would all
#       ride along. The exclusions below are therefore a security control, not
#       a build-speed optimisation.
#    2. NON-DETERMINISM. `.git` and `.venv` change constantly and have nothing
#       to do with the oracle. Excluding them makes the context -- and so the
#       build cache -- a function of the frozen sources and the pinned manifests
#       alone, which is what "two runs must be byte-identical" requires of every
#       layer of the harness.
#
#  DENY-BY-DEFAULT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* USED
#  ------------------------------------------
#  A `*` + re-include design is tempting but would be wrong here: the frozen
#  COBOL tree is the specification, `harness/build_oracle.sh` copies /repo to
#  /build wholesale, and a re-include list would silently drop a frozen
#  directory the day one is added. This file therefore names what must NEVER
#  enter the context and leaves the frozen sources alone.
#
#  WHAT IS DELIBERATELY *NOT* EXCLUDED, AND WHY
#  --------------------------------------------
#  Four large repository directories remain in the context -- `Basic-Code/`
#  (~89 MB), `ACAS-Manuals/` (~81 MB), `home/` (~44 MB) and `payroll/` (~35 MB)
#  -- and that is a deliberate decision, not an oversight. Excluding them would
#  shrink the context from roughly 270 MB to about 21 MB, so the temptation is
#  real and the reason for resisting it needs stating.
#
#  `harness/build_oracle.sh` already considered exactly this and decided against
#  it, in terms that apply here too [harness/build_oracle.sh:L1337-L1344]:
#  excluding directories to save space "would work today but would silently
#  break the moment a frozen script grew a reference", and "the faithful replica
#  is worth more than the disk". That decision is not reversed here.
#
#  Two further reasons specific to this file:
#
#    * The security objective is ALREADY met without touching them. Every one of
#      the four is public repository content -- COBOL-era BASIC sources, PDF
#      manuals, a vendored Berkeley DB tarball and an out-of-scope subsystem.
#      None is a credential, a key or a virtual environment. The measured result
#      of the exclusions above is ZERO credential-shaped files in the context.
#    * Excluding them would plant a latent trap. Today neither Dockerfile does
#      `COPY . `, so the context size only affects transfer time. If a future
#      change ever made an image self-contained by copying the tree, an excluded
#      directory would silently produce an INCOMPLETE replica -- precisely the
#      failure mode the build script warns about, and one that would surface as
#      a mysterious compile error rather than as a missing-file message.
#
#  So the ~270 MB is a known, documented trade: transfer cost in exchange for a
#  context that is a faithful subset of the checkout. What this file removes is
#  the material that is either SENSITIVE or genuinely UNRELATED to the build.
#
#  MUST NOT BE EXCLUDED -- THE BUILDS FAIL WITHOUT THESE
#  ----------------------------------------------------
#  Do not add patterns covering any of the following; each is a COPY source:
#
#      mysql/ACASDB.sql                   [harness/Dockerfile.mariadb:L301]
#      requirements.txt                   [harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol]
#      presql2-latest.zip                 [harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol]
#      mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip  [harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol]
#
#  The negated patterns at the end of this file re-include those six paths
#  explicitly, so that a future broad exclusion cannot break a build silently.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  VERSION CONTROL
#
#  ~158 MB here, and the single largest disclosure surface: the history carries
#  every version of every file ever committed, which is strictly more than the
#  checkout the images need.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.git
.git/**
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.gitmodules
.github


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  PYTHON VIRTUAL ENVIRONMENTS AND BUILD OUTPUT
#
#  ~122 MB, host-specific, and actively harmful: a virtual environment carries
#  absolute interpreter paths and platform-specific compiled extensions. The
#  image installs the Python side from the hashed lock files instead, so a
#  copied venv could only ever shadow the audited set.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.venv
.venv/**
venv
venv/**
env
env/**
ENV
.tox
.nox
*.egg-info
*.egg-info/**
.eggs
build
dist
wheels
pip-wheel-metadata
site-packages


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  PYTHON BYTECODE AND TOOL CACHES
#
#  Bytecode is stale by definition inside an image built from source, and
#  `__pycache__` directories defeat layer caching for no benefit.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__
__pycache__/**
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so.cache
.pytest_cache
.mypy_cache
.ruff_cache
.pytype
.dmypy.json
.coverage
.coverage.*
coverage.xml
htmlcov
.hypothesis


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  CREDENTIALS AND LOCAL CONFIGURATION -- THE PRIMARY SEC CONCERN
#
#  None of these is required by any image, and every one of them is a
#  credential-shaped path. `harness/docker-compose.yml` supplies the database
#  account through interpolated variables at RUN time and neither Dockerfile
#  declares a password ENV, precisely so that no credential reaches an image
#  layer; excluding these keeps the same promise for the CONTEXT.
#
#  `acas.param` and `presql2.param` are named explicitly because the ACAS
#  toolchain stores DBUSER/DBPASS in them in cleartext: the runtime copies live
#  outside the checkout (in $HOME/ACAS and the build tree), but an operator who
#  has ever copied one into the tree must not have it swept into a build.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.env
.env.*
*.env
!.env.example
.envrc
.direnv
secrets
secrets/**
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.jks
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_rsa*
id_dsa*
id_ecdsa*
id_ed25519*
*.ppk
.netrc
.npmrc
.pypirc
.my.cnf
.mylogin.cnf
acas.param
presql2.param
**/acas.param
**/presql2.param
.aws
.ssh
.gnupg
.docker/config.json
.bash_history
.zsh_history
.python_history


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  AGENT AND SESSION SCRATCH
#
#  ~3.8 MB of validation scratch from the migration work. Never an input to any
#  image, and it may contain probe fixtures carrying throwaway credentials.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.blitzy_tmp
.blitzy_tmp/**
blitzy_adhoc_test_*
**/blitzy_adhoc_test_*
.blitzyignore


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  VENDORED ARCHIVE THAT NEITHER IMAGE USES
#
#  `harness/Dockerfile.gnucobol` builds the client library from
#  `mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip` ONLY. The MySQL Connector/C tarball is
#  recorded in the plan's toolchain inventory as a vendored input but is not a
#  COPY source for either image, so it is 3.5 MB of context for nothing.
#
#  It is excluded by exact name rather than by a `*.tar.gz` glob, so that the
#  exclusion cannot silently widen if another archive is vendored later.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
mysql-connector-c-6.1.11-src.tar.gz


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  COMPILED COBOL OUTPUT AND HARNESS RUN ARTIFACTS
#
#  The oracle is compiled INSIDE the container into /build, never in the
#  checkout -- that is the whole reason /repo is mounted read-only
#  [harness/docker-compose.yml]. Any such file present in the tree is a stray
#  from a mistaken host-side build and must not be shipped into the context,
#  where it could shadow a freshly compiled module.
#
#  `*.prn` is listed because every frozen compile passes `-T <name>.prn`
#  [common/comp-common.sh:L26]; `cobmysqlapi.o` because the frozen recipe links
#  it as a bare filename resolved against the current directory, so a stray copy
#  in the context is exactly the kind of thing that produces a build that
#  "works" for the wrong reason.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.o
*.so
*.a
*.lst
*.prn
*.i.*
cobmysqlapi.o
**/cobmysqlapi.o
fh-logger.txt
**/fh-logger.txt
ACAS-Sysout.log
**/ACAS-Sysout.log
*.dat.bak
core
core.*


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  HARNESS OUTPUT TREES
#
#  Dumps, normalised dumps and diff evidence are produced into the /out volume
#  at run time. They carry LIVE ACCOUNTING DATA extracted from the database and
#  are written 0600 by the harness tools for that reason, so they must never be
#  swept into a build context either.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
out
out/**
dumps
dumps/**
*.dump
*.norm
run-logs
run-logs/**


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  EDITOR, OS AND ARCHIVE NOISE
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.idea
.vscode
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
.DS_Store
._*
.Spotlight-V100
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.log
!ACAS-Manuals/**/*.log


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  DOCKER'S OWN FILES
#
#  The Dockerfiles are read by the daemon from the path given by `dockerfile:`
#  and do not need to be part of the context payload. This file excludes itself
#  for the same reason.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
.dockerignore
docker-compose.override.yml
docker-compose.*.override.yml


# =============================================================================
#  EXPLICIT RE-INCLUSIONS -- THE SIX COPY SOURCES, MADE UNBREAKABLE
#
#  Docker applies the LAST matching pattern, so these negations override any
#  broader exclusion above and would also override a careless one added later.
#  Every path here is a COPY source in one of the two Dockerfiles; if you remove
#  a line from this block, that image stops building.
# =============================================================================
!mysql/ACASDB.sql
!requirements.txt
!presql2-latest.zip
!mariadb-connector-c-3.3.4-src.zip
